I am trying to upgrade a Java code base which parses groovy code that users give to be compatible with the Groovy parrot parser, and I am struggling.
Before parrot, we would catch syntax errors like this:
@Test(expected = MultipleCompilationErrorsException.class)
    public void testFailingOldWay() {
        CompilerConfiguration config = new CompilerConfiguration();
        config.setPluginFactory(ParserPluginFactory.antlr2());
        new GroovyShell(config).parse("this is not valid");
    }

with antlr4 / parrot, that code actually doesn't throw any parsing errors. At first I thought this was a bug, but I understand now, that it is possible to pass in dynamic methods and variables that it could somehow be valid
@Test
public void testFailingNewWayDoesNotWork() {
    CompilerConfiguration config = new CompilerConfiguration();
    // Expect the next line to throw a compilation error, but it doesn't.
    new GroovyShell(config).parse("this is not valid");
}

I have a list of variables that are to be available to the scripts provided, so I thought I would do something like:
@Test(expected = MultipleCompilationErrorsException.class)
public void testNewIdea() {
    CompilerConfiguration config = new CompilerConfiguration();
    config.addCompilationCustomizers(new ASTTransformationCustomizer(TypeChecked.class));
    new GroovyShell(config).parse("this is not valid");
}

This works, but I can't find a way to tell it to allow certain variables to be undefined.
I read the docs, and looked at this stack overflow post:
Embedded Groovy: How to use static type checking with external variables?
but the docs only seem to get me half way for the java part, and the post is all about doing it in groovy, which I am not.
In Java, the extensions parameter doesn't seem to exist.
I think that I need to make a custom annotation and a custom type checking extension, but I am not sure exactly.
My plan was this:
import org.codehaus.groovy.transform.GroovyASTTransformationClass;

@Target({ ElementType.TYPE, ElementType.METHOD })
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@GroovyASTTransformationClass("flexagon.fd.model.script.AllowUnresolvedVariables")
