Background
For simplicity, just using ADD as an example.
In compiler backend, multiple addition is organized by combinations of multiple ADD instructions. For example ADD(1, ADD(1,3)).
But since it is associative and commutative, then it doesn't matter about the order and which 2 add first. Thus it can be seen as equivalent to ADD(1, 1, 3).
The problem lies on algorithm of searching the tree to find a valid subtree of pattern ADD(operand$0, operand$1, ...).
What I think
I'm thinking of the algorithm as follow:
bool try_switch(
    std::vector<NODE> &ret, 
    std::vector<NODE> &ops, 
    std::vector<NODE> &possible_shrink, 
    std::vector<NODE> &possible_grow
)
{
    for (NODE shrink ： possible_shrink) {
        for (NODE grow ： possible_grow) {
            // avoid duplicate
            if (!is_left_to_right(shrink, grow)) continue;
            swap_nodes(ret, shrink, grow);
            if (sub_nodes_match(ret, ops)) {
                return true;
            }
            // Due to the change of picked node, the possiblity of further swich will be adjusted
            Adjust_possible(shrink, grow, possible_shrink, possible_grow)
            if(try_switch(ret, ops, &possible_shrink, &possible_grow)) {
                return ture;
            }
            // Revert the adjustment
            swap_nodes(ret, grow, shrink);
            Adjust_possible(grow, shrink, possible_shrink, possible_grow)
        }
    }
    return false;
}
bool match_ADD(int num_of_ops, std::vector<NODE> ops) 
{
    std::vector<NODE> possible_shrink;
    std::vector<NODE> possible_grow;
    std::vector<NODE> ret = find_n_mins_one_ADD(num_of_ops, &possible_shrink, &possible_grow);
    if (sub_nodes_match(ret, ops)) {
        return true;
    }

    return try_switch(ret, ops, &possible_shrink, &possible_grow);
}

The key problem is that for some possible tree nodes, they can be an ADD or be an operand of ADD(operand$0, operand$1, ...).
What I'm thinking is to matching operand$0,...,operand$n I need exactly $n-1$ node of ADD in one sub-tree and matching all the leaves of this sub-tree to the targeted operand$i, if all of them matches, then the matching successes.
If not, I need to give up one node in founded sub-tree and replace it with new ADD node to form a new sub-tree, and check if all the leafs of the current sub-tree matches the operand$0, operand$1, ...
Also by doing the swap of nodes, it creates or removes new possibilities, thus I need to do a recursive call to fall back and try other possibilities.
Example
Assume we have a tree:
ADD(
    ADD(1,int_reg3),
    ADD(
        3,
        SUB(int_reg1, int_reg2)
    )
)

And we are trying to match a pattern ADD(int_val, int_val, SUB(int_val, int_val)) (register is also an int_val)
The algorithm will return:

ret=[1, int_reg3, ADD(3, SUB(int_reg1, int_reg2))]
possible_shrink = [ADD(1, int_reg3)]
possible_grow = [ADD(3, SUB(int_reg1, int_reg2))]

Now ret does not match the target pattern since there is no SUB
Then we will try another ADD node and remove one from current.
The only possibility of ADD node can remove from current sub-tree is ADD(1, int_reg3), and the only possible node can be added is ADD(3, SUB(int_reg1, int_reg2))
Thus we have:

ret=[ADD(1, int_reg3), 3, SUB(int_reg1, int_reg2)]
possible_shrink = [ADD(3, SUB(int_reg1, int_reg2)]
possible_grow = [ADD(1, int_reg3)]

Now the situation is:

ret matches the pattern ,return ture
